          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does guidance describe as initial management for reflux before any investigation?",
          "answer": "Guidance describes a two-part approach for adults with typical reflux symptoms (heartburn and regurgitation) and no alarm features. The first part is lifestyle change. The second is a time-limited trial of acid-suppressing treatment, given before any investigation. The source is the GERD empiric management guideline note (*GERD: empiric initial management and the alarm features that change it*) and the general GERD reference note.\n\n**Lifestyle measures**\n- Losing weight, if body weight is above the healthy range.\n- Raising the head of the bed for night-time symptoms.\n- Not eating within two to three hours of lying down.\n- Stopping smoking.\n- Avoiding the specific foods that set off your own symptoms, rather than cutting out a long list of foods. The lifestyle note says broad elimination diets are poorly supported.\n\n**Empiric acid-suppressing trial**\nThe guideline describes an eight-week trial of a proton pump inhibitor (a class of acid-reducing medicine), taken before a meal, rather than investigation first. The guideline notes that timing matters, since a dose taken with or after food is a common reason for apparent non-response. It also says the trial should have a defined period and a planned review, not be open-ended. Doses are outside what I can give, so ask a clinician or pharmacist about starting or using any medicine.\n\n**When guidance says not to wait**\nPrompt investigation, rather than an empiric trial, is advised if any of these are present:\n- Difficulty or pain when swallowing.\n- Unintentional weight loss.\n- Vomiting blood or black, tarry stools.\n- Iron-deficiency anaemia.\n- Persistent vomiting.\n- A lump, or a family history of upper gastrointestinal cancer.\n- New symptoms starting at an older age.\n\n**Where authorities differ**\nThe retrieved material did not contain a disagreement about initial reflux management. The only disagreement it returned concerned hypertension, so I can't report one for reflux.\n\n**How strong the evidence is**\nThe retrieved passages do not give formal evidence grades for these recommendations. Two points are stated:\n- Weight reduction has the strongest supporting evidence of the lifestyle measures.\n- Blanket removal of trigger foods has weak evidence, and the passage says individual triggers vary a lot between people.\n\nFor the strength of evidence behind the eight-week acid-suppression trial, the retrieved material says nothing, so I can't say.\n\n**Safety**\nChest discomfort should not be assumed to be reflux. Seek emergency care straight away for chest discomfort that comes on with exertion, or comes with breathlessness, sweating, nausea, or pain spreading to the arm, neck or jaw, especially if you have heart risk factors. Heartburn and cardiac pain can feel the same.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
